  "response": "**Setting up Bayes' theorem:**\n\n- Prevalence: P(D) = 1/500 = 0.002, so P(¬D) = 0.998\n- Sensitivity: P(+|D) = 0.98\n- Specificity: P(¬+|¬D) = 0.95, so P(+|¬D) = 0.05\n\n**Calculating P(+):**\n\nP(+) = P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|¬D)·P(¬D)\nP(+) = (0.98 × 0.002) + (0.05 × 0.998)\nP(+) = 0.00196 + 0.04990\nP(+) = 0.05186\n\n**Applying Bayes' theorem:**\n\nP(D|+) = P(+|D)·P(D) / P(+) = 0.00196 / 0.05186 ≈ 0.037794\n\nSo about **3.78%** — despite testing positive, this person is still unlikely (roughly 96% chance) to actually have the condition, because the condition is rare and false positives from the healthy majority outnumber the true positives.\n\nFINAL ANSWER: 3.78",
